Okay, let's learn how to read a CSV file using JavaScript.

Since you're new to JavaScript, it's important to understand *where* JavaScript runs. The most common places are:

1.  **In a web browser:** This is often where people start. JavaScript interacts with HTML and the user's browser.
2.  **On a server (using Node.js):** This is for building back-end applications.

Reading files works differently in these two environments due to security reasons. In a browser, JavaScript cannot just access any file on the user's computer directly. You need the user to *select* the file.

We'll focus on the **browser method** first, as it's a very common way to handle user-provided files and is often the first scenario beginners encounter.

---

### Method 1: Reading a CSV in a Web Browser (Requires User Interaction)

This method involves:
1.  An HTML element that allows the user to select a file (`<input type="file">`).
2.  JavaScript code that listens for when a file is selected.
3.  Using the built-in `FileReader` object to read the content of the selected file.
4.  Parsing the text content (which is the CSV data).

**Step 1: Create the HTML**

You need a simple HTML page with a file input element.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Read CSV File</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Select a CSV File</h1>

    <!-- This is the file input element -->
    <input type="file" id="csvFile" accept=".csv">

    <!-- This div is optional, we can use it to display some output -->
    <div id="output"></div>

    <!-- Link to our JavaScript file -->
    <script src="script.js"></script>

</body>
</html>
```

*   `<input type="file" id="csvFile" accept=".csv">`: This creates a button that, when clicked, opens the user's file explorer. `id="csvFile"` gives it a name so we can find it in JavaScript. `accept=".csv"` is a hint to the browser to only show CSV files, though the user can usually override this.
*   `<div id="output">`: A place to show messages or data later.
*   `<script src="script.js"></script>`: This tells the browser to load and run the JavaScript code from a file named `script.js`. Make sure you create this file in the same folder as your HTML.

**Step 2: Write the JavaScript (`script.js`)**

Now, let's write the JavaScript code to handle the file selection and reading.

```javascript
// Get references to the HTML elements
const fileInput = document.getElementById('csvFile');
const outputDiv = document.getElementById('output');

// Add an event listener to the file input
// This function will run when the user selects a file
fileInput.addEventListener('change', function(event) {

    // Get the selected file from the input
    // event.target.files is an array of files (usually just one for type="file")
    const file = event.target.files[0];

    // Check if a file was actually selected
    if (file) {
        // Create a new FileReader object
        // FileReader is a built-in browser object to read file contents
        const reader = new FileReader();

        // Define what happens when the file is successfully read
        // The reading is asynchronous, so this function runs *later*
        reader.onload = function(e) {
            // e.target.result contains the content of the file as a string
            const fileContent = e.target.result;

            // --- Now, parse the CSV content ---
            // CSV is basically text separated by commas and new lines

            // 1. Split the content into lines
            //    .split('\n') divides the string wherever it sees a newline character
            const lines = fileContent.split('\n');

            // Let's assume the first line is headers (column names)
            const headers = lines[0].split(',');

            // Array to store our parsed data as objects (more useful than just arrays)
            const data = [];

            // Loop through the lines, starting from the second line (index 1)
            // because the first line is headers
            for (let i = 1; i < lines.length; i++) {
                const currentLine = lines[i];

                // Skip empty lines
                if (currentLine.trim() === '') {
                    continue;
                }

                // 2. Split the line into values (columns)
                //    .split(',') divides the line wherever it sees a comma
                const values = currentLine.split(',');

                // Basic check: ensure the number of values matches the number of headers
                // This helps catch simple errors but isn't foolproof for complex CSVs
                if (values.length === headers.length) {
                    const row = {}; // Create an empty object for this row

                    // Loop through the values and assign them to the header names
                    for (let j = 0; j < headers.length; j++) {
                        // Use the header name as the key and the value as the value
                        // .trim() removes leading/trailing whitespace from headers and values
                        row[headers[j].trim()] = values[j].trim();
                    }

                    data.push(row); // Add the row object to our data array

                } else {
                    // Handle lines that don't have the expected number of columns
                    console.warn(`Skipping malformed line ${i + 1}: ${currentLine}`);
                }
            }

            // --- You now have the data in the 'data' array ---
            // Each element in 'data' is an object representing a row
            // The keys of the object are the column headers

            console.log('Headers:', headers); // Log headers to the browser's console
            console.log('Parsed Data (as objects):', data); // Log the data array

            // You can now use this data, e.g., display it on the page
            outputDiv.innerHTML = `<p>CSV file "${file.name}" read successfully.</p>
                                   <p>Number of rows (excluding header): ${data.length}</p>`;

            // Example: Display the first few rows in the output div
            if (data.length > 0) {
                 let displayHtml = '<h2>First 5 Rows:</h2><ul>';
                 for(let k = 0; k < Math.min(5, data.length); k++){
                     displayHtml += `<li>${JSON.stringify(data[k])}</li>`; // Convert object to string for display
                 }
                 displayHtml += '</ul>';
                 outputDiv.innerHTML += displayHtml;
            }


        };

        // Define what happens if there's an error reading the file
        reader.onerror = function(e) {
            console.error('Error reading file:', e.target.error);
            outputDiv.innerHTML = `<p>Error reading file: ${e.target.error.name}</p>`;
        };

        // Start reading the file as plain text
        reader.readAsText(file);

    } else {
        // If no file was selected
        outputDiv.innerHTML = '<p>No file selected.</p>';
    }
});
```

**Step 3: Open the HTML File**

Save the HTML code as `index.html` and the JavaScript code as `script.js` in the *same folder*. Then, open `index.html` in your web browser.

You will see a button like "Choose File". Click it, select a `.csv` file from your computer, and the JavaScript will read it and print the parsed data to your browser's developer console (usually opened by pressing F12 and going to the "Console" tab). It will also show a summary on the page.

---

### Explanation of the JavaScript Code

1.  **`const fileInput = document.getElementById('csvFile');`**
    *   `document.getElementById('csvFile')` finds the HTML element with the ID `csvFile` (our `<input type="file">`).
    *   `const` declares a variable that cannot be reassigned. We store the found element in the `fileInput` variable.

2.  **`const outputDiv = document.getElementById('output');`**
    *   Gets the `div` element with the ID `output`.

3.  **`fileInput.addEventListener('change', function(event) { ... });`**
    *   `addEventListener` is how you make JavaScript react to things happening on an HTML element (events).
    *   We're listening for the `'change'` event on the `fileInput`. This event fires when the user selects a file.
    *   The second argument is a function that will run when the event happens. This function automatically receives an `event` object with details about the event.

4.  **`const file = event.target.files[0];`**
    *   When the `'change'` event happens on a file input, `event.target` refers to the input element itself.
    *   `event.target.files` is a list (specifically, a `FileList`) of the files the user selected. Since our input doesn't allow multiple files, there will be only one file at index `0`.
    *   We store the selected `File` object in the `file` variable.

5.  **`if (file) { ... }`**
    *   Checks if a file was actually selected (the user might cancel the file selection dialog).

6.  **`const reader = new FileReader();`**
    *   Creates a new instance of the built-in `FileReader` object. This object is specifically designed to read the contents of `File` or `Blob` objects.

7.  **`reader.onload = function(e) { ... };`**
    *   `FileReader` operations are *asynchronous*. This means `reader.readAsText(file)` (which we call later) *starts* the reading process but doesn't wait for it to finish. The rest of your code continues running.
    *   The `onload` property is set to a function that will be executed *only after* the `FileReader` has successfully finished reading the file.
    *   The function receives an event `e`. `e.target.result` contains the *result* of the reading operation, which is the file's content as a string when using `readAsText`.

8.  **`const fileContent = e.target.result;`**
    *   Gets the text content of the CSV file.

9.  **`const lines = fileContent.split('\n');`**
    *   The core of simple CSV parsing. It takes the entire text string and splits it into an array of strings, where each element is one line from the CSV. It uses the newline character (`\n`) as the separator. (Note: Windows uses `\r\n`, so a more robust split might be `split(/\r?\n/)` but `\n` often works well enough as browsers normalize line endings).

10. **`const headers = lines[0].split(',');`**
    *   Assumes the *first* line (`lines[0]`) contains the column headers. It splits this line by commas to get an array of header names.

11. **`const data = [];`**
    *   Initializes an empty array where we will store the parsed data. We'll store each row as an object for easier access (e.g., `row.Name` instead of `row[0]`).

12. **`for (let i = 1; i < lines.length; i++) { ... }`**
    *   Starts a loop to process each line *after* the header row (hence `i = 1`).

13. **`const values = currentLine.split(',');`**
    *   Inside the loop, each `currentLine` (which is a string) is split by commas to get an array of the values in that row.

14. **`if (values.length === headers.length) { ... }`**
    *   A basic check to make sure the current line has the same number of columns as the header row. This helps prevent errors if a line is malformed or empty (though empty lines are often handled by the `trim() === ''` check).

15. **`const row = {};`**
    *   Creates an empty object to represent the current row's data.

16. **`for (let j = 0; j < headers.length; j++) { ... }`**
    *   Loops through the columns of the current row.

17. **`row[headers[j].trim()] = values[j].trim();`**
    *   This is key:
        *   `headers[j]` gets the j-th header name (e.g., "Name", "Age"). `.trim()` removes any extra spaces around the header name.
        *   `values[j]` gets the j-th value from the current line. `.trim()` removes any extra spaces around the value.
        *   `row[...something...] = ...value...;` assigns the value to a property on the `row` object. The property name is taken from the header.
        *   So, if `headers[0]` is "Name" and `values[0]` is "Alice", this line does `row["Name"] = "Alice";`.

18. **`data.push(row);`**
    *   Adds the completed `row` object to the `data` array.

19. **`console.log(...)` and `outputDiv.innerHTML = ...`**
    *   These lines show you how to use the `data` array after it's parsed. We print it to the browser's developer console and update the content of the `outputDiv` on the webpage.

20. **`reader.onerror = function(e) { ... };`**
    *   Similar to `onload`, this function runs if there's an error during the file reading process (e.g., permission denied, file not found - though in this user-selection context, errors are less common).

21. **`reader.readAsText(file);`**
    *   This is the command that actually *starts* the reading process. It tells the `FileReader` to read the content of the `file` object and interpret it as text.

22. **`else { ... }`**
    *   This block runs if the initial `if (file)` check is false, meaning the user didn't select a file.

---

### Important Considerations & Limitations

*   **Simple Parsing:** The parsing code (`split('\n')` and `split(',')`) is very basic. It *will not* correctly handle CSV files where:
    *   Values contain commas (e.g., `"Smith, John"`).
    *   Values contain newlines (e.g., `"Line 1\nLine 2"`).
    *   Values contain quotes (e.g., `"He said ""Hello!""`).
    *   The delimiter is not a comma (e.g., tab-separated values or semicolons).
*   **Robust Parsing:** For real-world CSV files, especially those coming from various sources, you should use a dedicated CSV parsing library. A very popular and excellent one for JavaScript (both browser and Node.js) is **PapaParse**. It handles all the quoting, escaping, different delimiters, etc., correctly.
*   **Large Files:** Reading a very large file entirely into memory as a single string (`reader.readAsText`) might consume a lot of memory and make the browser unresponsive. For very large files, more advanced techniques (like streaming or using a library that supports streaming) are needed.

---

### Method 2: Reading a CSV in Node.js (Server-Side)

If you are working with Node.js (on a server or for scripting), you can read files directly from the file system using the built-in `fs` (File System) module.

```javascript
// In Node.js, you need to require modules
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path'); // Helps work with file paths

// Define the path to your CSV file
// Make sure this file exists in the same directory or provide the full path
const csvFilePath = path.join(__dirname, 'mydata.csv'); // Example: 'mydata.csv' in the same folder

fs.readFile(csvFilePath, 'utf8', (err, fileContent) => {
    if (err) {
        console.error('Error reading the file:', err);
        return; // Stop execution if there's an error
    }

    // fileContent now holds the CSV data as a string

    // --- Simple CSV Parsing (Same logic as browser example) ---
    const lines = fileContent.split('\n');
    const headers = lines[0].split(',');
    const data = [];

    for (let i = 1; i < lines.length; i++) {
        const currentLine = lines[i];
        if (currentLine.trim() === '') {
            continue;
        }
        const values = currentLine.split(',');

        if (values.length === headers.length) {
            const row = {};
            for (let j = 0; j < headers.length; j++) {
                 row[headers[j].trim()] = values[j].trim();
            }
            data.push(row);
        } else {
            console.warn(`Skipping malformed line ${i + 1}: ${currentLine}`);
        }
    }

    // --- Use the parsed data ---
    console.log('Headers:', headers);
    console.log('Parsed Data (as objects):', data);

    // You can now process the data further (e.g., store in a database, perform calculations)
});

// Note: Because fs.readFile is asynchronous, this console.log
// will likely appear *before* the file reading is finished and the data is logged.
console.log('Attempting to read file...');
```

**Explanation of Node.js Code:**

1.  **`const fs = require('fs');`**
    *   Imports the built-in Node.js File System module.
2.  **`const path = require('path');`**
    *   Imports the built-in Node.js Path module, useful for creating file paths that work correctly across different operating systems.
3.  **`const csvFilePath = path.join(__dirname, 'mydata.csv');`**
    *   `__dirname` is a Node.js global variable that gives you the directory path of the currently executing script.
    *   `path.join` combines directory segments into a single path. This creates the full path to `mydata.csv` assuming it's in the same directory as your script.
4.  **`fs.readFile(csvFilePath, 'utf8', (err, fileContent) => { ... });`**
    *   `fs.readFile` is the function to read a file.
    *   The first argument is the path to the file.
    *   The second argument `'utf8'` specifies the character encoding.
    *   The third argument is a *callback function* that will run *after* the file has been read. This is Node.js's common way of handling asynchronous operations.
        *   The callback function receives two arguments: `err` (an error object if something went wrong) and `fileContent` (the content of the file as a string if successful).
5.  **`if (err) { ... }`**
    *   Checks if there was an error during reading.
6.  **`fileContent`**
    *   This variable holds the entire text content of the CSV file, just like in the browser example.
7.  **Parsing Logic:** The parsing logic (`split`, loops, creating objects) is exactly the same as the browser example because you're just working with a string.
8.  **`console.log(...)`**
    *   Prints output to the Node.js console where you run the script.

To run this:
1.  Save the code as a `.js` file (e.g., `read_node.js`).
2.  Create a `mydata.csv` file in the *same folder*.
3.  Open your terminal or command prompt.
4.  Navigate to that folder.
5.  Run the script using `node read_node.js`.

---

### Conclusion

For a beginner working in the browser, the `FileReader` method with an `<input type="file">` is the standard way to read files selected by the user.

The simple `split()` method for parsing is good for understanding the basic structure of CSV, but for any serious work, you should definitely use a library like **PapaParse** which handles the complexities of the CSV format robustly.

Choose the method (Browser or Node.js) based on where your JavaScript code is running and where the CSV file is located (on the user's computer vs. on the server).

Good luck with your JavaScript learning journey!

Okay, here is a simple JavaScript function that extracts the visible text content from the main body of a web document.

It primarily uses the `textContent` property, which is generally the most reliable and performant way to get the raw text from an element and its descendants, ignoring HTML tags and most styling.

```javascript
/**
 * Extracts all text content from the document body.
 *
 * @returns {string} The combined text content of the document body,
 *                   or an empty string if the body is not available.
 */
function extractDocumentText() {
  // Check if the document body exists (it should in a loaded HTML page)
  if (document.body) {
    // Use textContent to get all text nodes within the body, ignoring HTML tags.
    // It's generally preferred over innerText for raw text extraction
    // as it's faster and doesn't depend on CSS rendering.
    return document.body.textContent || ''; // Return empty string if textContent is null/undefined
  } else {
    // Return an empty string if the body element is not found
    // (e.g., script ran before the DOM was fully parsed)
    console.warn("Document body not found. Cannot extract text.");
    return '';
  }
}

// --- How to use it ---

// You would typically call this function after the document is ready.
// For example, inside a script tag at the end of the body, or after
// the 'DOMContentLoaded' event fires.

// Example usage (run this after the HTML body is loaded):
// const allText = extractDocumentText();
// console.log("Extracted text:", allText);

// Or, if you want to ensure the DOM is ready:
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const allText = extractDocumentText();
  console.log("--- Full Document Text ---");
  console.log(allText);
  console.log("------------------------");

  // You can also get the text of a specific element:
  // const someElement = document.getElementById('my-specific-div');
  // if (someElement) {
  //   console.log("\n--- Text from a specific element ---");
  //   console.log(someElement.textContent);
  //   console.log("----------------------------------");
  // }
});


/*
  Alternative using innerText:

  function extractDocumentTextWithInnerText() {
    if (document.body) {
      // innerText respects CSS visibility (won't include hidden elements)
      // and attempts to preserve some formatting (like line breaks).
      // It's generally slower than textContent because it requires layout information.
      return document.body.innerText || '';
    } else {
      console.warn("Document body not found. Cannot extract text.");
      return '';
    }
  }

  // Example usage:
  // document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  //   const visibleText = extractDocumentTextWithInnerText();
  //   console.log("--- Visible Document Text (using innerText) ---");
  //   console.log(visibleText);
  //   console.log("---------------------------------------------");
  // });
*/
```

**Explanation:**

1.  **`function extractDocumentText() { ... }`**: Defines a function named `extractDocumentText`.
2.  **`if (document.body)`**: Checks if the `<body>` element of the document is available. This is important because scripts can sometimes run before the entire HTML structure is parsed.
3.  **`document.body.textContent`**: This is the core part.
    *   `document.body` refers to the main content area of the HTML page.
    *   `.textContent` is a property available on all HTML elements. It returns the text content of the element *and all its child elements*, stripping out all HTML tags and preserving script/style content (though we're getting it from the `body`, so script/style tags *within* the body would have their *content* included, which might or might not be desired - usually, scripts and styles are in the `<head>` or their content is ignored by `textContent` on the body). It's fast and doesn't care if the text is visually hidden by CSS (except `display: none` on the element itself, but `document.body` is rarely `display: none`).
4.  **`|| ''`**: This is a safeguard. If `textContent` somehow returned `null` or `undefined` (unlikely for `document.body` in a loaded page but good practice), it defaults to an empty string.
5.  **`else { ... }`**: If `document.body` isn't found, it logs a warning and returns an empty string to prevent errors.
6.  **`document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', ...)`**: The example usage shows the best practice: wait for the `DOMContentLoaded` event. This event fires when the initial HTML has been completely loaded and parsed, and the DOM is fully built, ensuring `document.body` is available.

Choose between `textContent` and `innerText` based on your needs:
*   Use `textContent` for raw text extraction, including text that might be visually hidden but is still in the DOM (except `display: none`). It's faster.
*   Use `innerText` if you specifically want the text *as the user sees it*, respecting CSS visibility and basic formatting. It's slower. The provided function uses `textContent` as the default.

Okay, here is a Java code snippet using Streams to achieve the desired logic.

The core idea is to use the `reduce` stream operation. We can provide `null` as an initial identity. The accumulator function will keep track of the first element seen. If it encounters a second element when the accumulator is already holding the first element, it knows there's more than one and throws the exception. If the stream is empty, `reduce` returns the initial identity (`null`). If the stream has one element, the accumulator is called once, returns that element, and `reduce` returns that element.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects; // Optional: good practice for null checks if needed, but not strictly required for this reduce logic

public class ListSingleEntryChecker {

    /**
     * Checks a list for its size constraint using Java Streams.
     * - If the list is empty, returns null.
     * - If the list contains exactly one entry, returns that entry.
     * - If the list contains more than one entry, throws an IllegalArgumentException.
     *
     * @param list The list to check. Must not be null.
     * @param <T> The type of elements in the list.
     * @return The single entry if present, or null if the list is empty.
     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if the list contains more than one entry or if the input list is null.
     */
    public static <T> T getSingleEntryOrNull(List<T> list) {
        // Handle null input list explicitly before streaming
        if (list == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input list cannot be null");
        }

        // Use stream.reduce to process the list elements.
        // - The 'identity' is the initial value for the accumulation and the result for an empty stream.
        //   We use 'null' here, meaning an empty list results in null.
        // - The 'accumulator' is called for each element.
        //   - If 'acc' (the accumulated value so far) is null, it means this is the first element seen.
        //     We return the current 'item' to become the new accumulated value.
        //   - If 'acc' is not null, it means we've already seen at least one element (which is stored in 'acc').
        //     Encountering another 'item' means there's more than one element in total, so we throw an exception.
        T result = list.stream().reduce(
                null, // Identity: The value returned for an empty stream
                (acc, item) -> { // Accumulator: Processes each element 'item' with the current accumulated value 'acc'
                    if (acc == null) {
                        // This is the first element encountered in the stream
                        return item;
                    } else {
                        // We already have an accumulated value (acc), meaning we've seen at least one element before.
                        // Encountering 'item' now means there are multiple elements.
                        throw new IllegalArgumentException("List contains more than one entry.");
                    }
                }
                // Note: For sequential streams (like the default here), only the identity and accumulator are strictly
                // needed. A combiner is required for parallel streams, but the logic of detecting "more than one"
                // by seeing a second element doesn't map cleanly to a parallel combiner without extra state or complexity.
                // This solution is most naturally applied to sequential streams.
        );

        return result;
    }

    // --- Example Usage ---
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> emptyList = List.of();
        List<String> singleList = List.of("The only element");
        List<String> multiList = List.of("First", "Second");
        List<String> anotherMultiList = List.of("A", "B", "C");

        System.out.println("--- Checking empty list ---");
        try {
            String result = getSingleEntryOrNull(emptyList);
            System.out.println("Result: " + result); // Expected: null
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught exception: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("\n--- Checking single entry list ---");
        try {
            String result = getSingleEntryOrNull(singleList);
            System.out.println("Result: " + result); // Expected: "The only element"
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught exception: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("\n--- Checking multiple entry list (2 elements) ---");
        try {
            String result = getSingleEntryOrNull(multiList);
            System.out.println("Result: " + result);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught exception: " + e.getMessage()); // Expected: "List contains more than one entry."
        }

        System.out.println("\n--- Checking multiple entry list (>2 elements) ---");
        try {
            String result = getSingleEntryOrNull(anotherMultiList);
            System.out.println("Result: " + result);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught exception: " + e.getMessage()); // Expected: "List contains more than one entry."
        }

        System.out.println("\n--- Checking null list ---");
        try {
            String result = getSingleEntryOrNull(null);
            System.out.println("Result: " + result);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught exception: " + e.getMessage()); // Expected: "Input list cannot be null"
        }
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

1.  **`getSingleEntryOrNull(List<T> list)` method:** Takes a `List` of type `T`.
2.  **`if (list == null)`:** A basic null check for the input list itself, which is good practice before calling `stream()` on it.
3.  **`list.stream()`:** Creates a sequential stream from the list.
4.  **`.reduce(null, (acc, item) -> { ... })`:** This is the core Stream operation.
    *   `null`: This is the `identity`. It's the initial value of the accumulator (`acc`) and the value returned by `reduce` if the stream is empty. This handles the "no entry, return null" case.
    *   `(acc, item) -> { ... }`: This is the `accumulator` BiFunction. It takes the current accumulated value (`acc`) and the next element from the stream (`item`).
        *   `if (acc == null)`: If the accumulated value is `null`, it means this is the very first element (`item`) being processed. We return `item` to become the new accumulated value.
        *   `else`: If the accumulated value (`acc`) is *not* null, it means we have already successfully processed at least one element *before* encountering the current `item`. This signifies that the list has more than one element. We immediately throw an `IllegalArgumentException` to signal this error condition.

This approach fulfills all requirements by leveraging the `reduce` operation's behavior for empty streams, single-element streams, and its ability to process elements sequentially, allowing us to detect the second element and throw an exception.

Okay, here is a function for a discrete-time Kalman filter implemented in GNU Octave, designed to process a sequence of measurements.

This function takes the system matrices, noise covariances, and the measurement sequence (and optional control input sequence) as inputs and returns the sequence of state estimates and their error covariances.

```octave
function [x_hat_seq, P_seq, x_hat_pred_seq, P_pred_seq, K_seq] = kalman_filter_sequence(F, B, H, Q, R, z, u, x_hat_0, P_0)
    % KALMAN_FILTER_SEQUENCE Implements the discrete-time Kalman filter.
    %
    % [x_hat_seq, P_seq, x_hat_pred_seq, P_pred_seq, K_seq] = kalman_filter_sequence(F, B, H, Q, R, z, u, x_hat_0, P_0)
    %
    % This function processes a sequence of measurements using the standard
    % discrete-time Kalman filter equations.
    %
    % Inputs:
    %   F           - State transition matrix (n x n)
    %   B           - Control input matrix (n x m). Can be [] if no control input.
    %   H           - Measurement matrix (p x n)
    %   Q           - Process noise covariance matrix (n x n)
    %   R           - Measurement noise covariance matrix (p x p)
    %   z           - Sequence of measurements (p x N), where N is the number of time steps
    %   u           - Sequence of control inputs (m x N). Can be [] or zeros(m, N) if no control.
    %   x_hat_0     - Initial state estimate (n x 1) at time step 0
    %   P_0         - Initial error covariance matrix (n x n) at time step 0
    %
    % Outputs:
    %   x_hat_seq   - Sequence of estimated states (n x N) after measurement update
    %   P_seq       - Sequence of error covariance matrices (n x n x N) after measurement update
    %   x_hat_pred_seq - Sequence of predicted states (n x N) before measurement update
    %   P_pred_seq  - Sequence of predicted error covariance matrices (n x n x N) before measurement update
    %   K_seq       - Sequence of Kalman gain matrices (n x p x N) for each step
    %
    % State Space Model:
    %   x(k) = F * x(k-1) + B * u(k) + w(k)   (Process model)
    %   z(k) = H * x(k) + v(k)               (Measurement model)
    %   w(k) ~ N(0, Q)                       (Process noise)
    %   v(k) ~ N(0, R)                       (Measurement noise)

    % --- Input Validation and Setup ---
    n = size(F, 1); % State dimension
    p = size(H, 1); % Measurement dimension
    N = size(z, 2); % Number of time steps (measurements)

    if size(z, 1) ~= p
        error('Measurement matrix H (%d rows) and measurement sequence z (%d rows) dimensions do not match.', p, size(z, 1));
    end
    if size(F, 2) ~= n || size(Q, 1) ~= n || size(Q, 2) ~= n || size(x_hat_0, 1) ~= n || size(P_0, 1) ~= n || size(P_0, 2) ~= n
        error('State dimension mismatch among F, Q, x_hat_0, or P_0.');
    end
    if size(R, 1) ~= p || size(R, 2) ~= p
        error('Measurement noise covariance R dimension mismatch.');
    end
    if size(H, 2) ~= n
         error('Measurement matrix H (%d columns) and state dimension (%d) mismatch.', size(H, 2), n);
    end

    has_control = ~isempty(B) && ~isempty(u);
    if has_control
         m = size(B, 2); % Control input dimension
         if size(B, 1) ~= n || size(u, 1) ~= m || size(u, 2) ~= N
             error('Control input dimensions mismatch among B, u, or z.');
         end
    else
         % Handle case with no control input gracefully by using dummy zero matrices
         % This avoids needing if/else inside the loop for B*u_k calculation
         B = zeros(n, 1); % Dummy matrix (size doesn't matter if u is zero)
         u = zeros(1, N); % Dummy vector sequence (size doesn't matter if B is zero)
         m = 1; % Dummy dimension
    end

    % --- Pre-allocate storage for results ---
    x_hat_seq = zeros(n, N);
    P_seq = zeros(n, n, N);
    x_hat_pred_seq = zeros(n, N);
    P_pred_seq = zeros(n, n, N);
    K_seq = zeros(n, p, N); % Store Kalman gains

    % --- Initialization ---
    % x_hat_k_k: State estimate at time k, given measurements up to k
    % P_k_k: Error covariance at time k, given measurements up to k
    x_hat_k_k = x_hat_0;
    P_k_k = P_0;

    I = eye(n); % Identity matrix for covariance update

    % --- Kalman Filter Loop ---
    for k = 1:N
        % Current measurement and control input
        z_k = z(:, k);
        if has_control
            u_k = u(:, k);
        else
            u_k = zeros(m, 1); % Use dummy zero vector if no control
        end

        % --- Prediction (Time Update) ---
        % Predict the state at time k using the model
        x_hat_k_km1 = F * x_hat_k_k + B * u_k; % x(k|k-1) = F*x(k-1|k-1) + B*u(k)

        % Predict the error covariance at time k
        P_k_km1 = F * P_k_k * F' + Q;         % P(k|k-1) = F*P(k-1|k-1)*F' + Q

        % Store predicted values
        x_hat_pred_seq(:, k) = x_hat_k_km1;
        P_pred_seq(:, :, k) = P_k_km1;

        % --- Update (Measurement Update) ---
        % Calculate the Innovation (or residual) covariance
        S_k = H * P_k_km1 * H' + R;

        % Calculate the Kalman Gain
        % Using left division (P_k_km1 * H') / S_k is generally preferred over
        % matrix inverse (P_k_km1 * H' * inv(S_k)) for numerical stability.
        K_k = (P_k_km1 * H') / S_k; % K(k) = P(k|k-1)*H' * inv(H*P(k|k-1)*H'+R)

        % Calculate the Innovation (or residual)
        y_k = z_k - H * x_hat_k_km1;

        % Update the state estimate using the measurement
        x_hat_k_k = x_hat_k_km1 + K_k * y_k; % x(k|k) = x(k|k-1) + K(k)*(z(k) - H*x(k|k-1))

        % Update the error covariance
        % Use the Joseph form for numerical stability (guarantees symmetry and positive semi-definiteness)
        P_k_k = (I - K_k * H) * P_k_km1 * (I - K_k * H)' + K_k * R * K_k';

        % A simpler, less stable form is: P_k_k = (I - K_k * H) * P_k_km1;

        % Store updated values and Kalman gain
        x_hat_seq(:, k) = x_hat_k_k;
        P_seq(:, :, k) = P_k_k;
        K_seq(:, :, k) = K_k;

    end % end for loop

end % end function
```

**How to use it (Example: Tracking a 1D object with constant velocity)**

Let's create a simple example to demonstrate the filter. We'll track an object's position and velocity in 1D, measuring only the position.

```octave
% --- Example Usage: 1D Constant Velocity Tracking ---

% 1. Define System Parameters
dt = 0.1; % Time step

% State vector: [position; velocity] (n=2)
F = [1, dt;
     0, 1]; % State transition matrix

B = []; % No control input in this simple example (m=0)
u = []; % No control input sequence

% Measurement vector: [position] (p=1)
H = [1, 0]; % Measurement matrix (we measure position)

% Process noise covariance (n x n)
% Assume small random acceleration causes velocity changes
sigma_a = 0.1; % Standard deviation of acceleration noise
Q = [dt^4/4, dt^3/2;
     dt^3/2, dt^2] * sigma_a^2; % Derivation from continuous-time white noise acceleration

% Measurement noise covariance (p x p)
sigma_z = 1.0; % Standard deviation of position measurement noise
R = sigma_z^2;

% 2. Generate Simulated Data
N = 100; % Number of time steps
true_x_0 = [0; 1]; % Initial true state [position; velocity]

true_x_seq = zeros(2, N);
z_seq = zeros(1, N);

% Add noise generators
process_noise = randn(2, N) .* sqrt(diag(Q)); % Simplified noise generation
measurement_noise = randn(1, N) * sigma_z;

true_x_k = true_x_0;
for k = 1:N
    % Simulate true state evolution (add process noise for realism)
    % In a real simulation, process noise is added *before* the measurement
    % and state update. Here, we'll add it to the true state for simplicity.
    if k > 1
        true_x_k = F * true_x_seq(:, k-1); % Propagate true state without process noise first
    end
    true_x_k = true_x_k + process_noise(:, k); % Add process noise

    true_x_seq(:, k) = true_x_k;

    % Simulate measurement
    z_k = H * true_x_k + measurement_noise(:, k);
    z_seq(:, k) = z_k;
end

% 3. Define Initial Conditions for the Filter
x_hat_0 = [0; 0]; % Initial guess for state (e.g., at origin, zero velocity)
P_0 = [100, 0;
       0, 100]; % Initial covariance (high uncertainty)

% 4. Run the Kalman Filter
[x_hat_seq, P_seq, x_hat_pred_seq, P_pred_seq, K_seq] = kalman_filter_sequence(F, B, H, Q, R, z_seq, u, x_hat_0, P_0);

% 5. Visualize Results
time = (1:N) * dt;

figure;

subplot(2,1,1);
plot(time, true_x_seq(1, :), 'b-', 'LineWidth', 1.5, 'DisplayName', 'True Position');
hold on;
plot(time, z_seq(1, :), 'rx', 'MarkerSize', 5, 'DisplayName', 'Measurements');
